00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:04.900
But in Puerto Rico, traditional Medicare fee for service is virtually non-existent.

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:08.340
Wait, I have to challenge this, because I really want you to explain the mechanics

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:09.340
of that to the listener.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:10.340
Okay, sure.

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:13.700
If I'm a senior living in Florida or Ohio, I can just hold up my red, white and blue

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:17.740
Medicare card, go to almost any doctor, and the federal government simply pays the

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:18.900
bill.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:22.620
You're saying that fundamental American safety net simply doesn't happen in Puerto

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:23.620
Rico.

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:24.780
How is that possible?

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:26.540
It is statistically negligible.

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:30.860
Over the years, due to complex demographic factors, distinct federal funding

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:35.220
formulas applied to the territories and aggressive market adaptations, almost every

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:39.580
single Medicare eligible senior in Puerto Rico has been enrolled into a privatized

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:41.180
Medicare Advantage Plan.

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:45.460
So the island essentially wholesale privatized its senior safety net.

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:46.460
Yes.

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:50.780
These privatized Advantage Plans and the Platino Plans completely shoulder the

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:53.040
burden of the aging population.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:56.500
They are the primary interface between the federal government's money and the

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:58.020
island's doctors.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:01.660
Let's define Platino for you, just so we are all speaking the exact same

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:02.660
language here.

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:03.660
Good idea.

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:07.340
Platino patients are what we call dual eligible.

00:15:07.340 --> 00:15:09.300
That means they are highly vulnerable.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:13.740
They qualify for Medicare due to their advanced age or severe disability.

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:17.540
And need they qualify for Medicaid because their income level falls below the

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:18.540
poverty line.

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:22.180
They are often the sickest of the sick, requiring immense resources.

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:26.060
But the way the system is engineered in Puerto Rico, these Platino patients

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:29.660
are functionally operating as Medicare Advantage patients.

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:33.380
The privatized Advantage companies are the ones managing their care, not the

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:34.660
local government.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:38.380
And when you look at the financial footprint of these plans and the OCS filings,

00:15:38.380 --> 00:15:40.380
the dominance is awe-inspiring.

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:41.900
Let's look at the 2025 numbers.

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:43.420
Well, let's hear them.

00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:48.540
Medicare Platinum represented 33.4% of the total premium in the entire market.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:52.500
That is $5.73 billion.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:59.460
Standard Medicare Advantage represented 19.0% of the premium, which is $3.27 billion.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:02.380
So if you do the math and you absolutely have to add them together

00:16:02.380 --> 00:16:06.580
because they are both managed under the exact same operational umbrellas by these

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:12.420
Advantage companies, you get over 52% of the entire health care money in the system.

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:13.420
Over half.

00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:15.180
$9 billion.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:20.260
Together, just those two segments control over half the money in the entire

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:22.260
Puerto Rican health care ecosystem.

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:26.100
This raises an important question, and it is perhaps the most critical

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:27.460
question of this deep dive.

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:28.020
What's that?

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:32.820
What happens to a society's health care ecosystem when one single financial

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:35.140
mechanism holds that much weight?

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:35.980
Think about it.

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:40.980
The entire hospital infrastructure, the vast networks of specialists,

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:43.500
the clinical laboratories processing blood work.

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:47.700
Their daily revenue stream is overwhelmingly existentially dependent

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:51.620
on the reimbursement rates set by these Medicare Advantage and Platino contracts.

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:52.780
Completely dependent.

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:56.060
So if traditional Medicare fee-for-service doesn't exist here,

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:58.900
then these Advantage plans aren't just an option like they are in the states.

00:16:58.900 --> 00:16:59.780
No, not at all.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:01.420
They aren't a luxury upgrade.

00:17:01.420 --> 00:17:04.100
They are the entire floorboards of the house.

00:17:04.100 --> 00:17:07.140
If the Advantage system sneezes, does the whole

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:10.100
island's health care system catch a catastrophic cold?

00:17:10.100 --> 00:17:11.340
That is not a metaphor.

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:13.420
It is an economic reality.

00:17:13.420 --> 00:17:16.820
If the federal funding for Medicare Advantage in Puerto Rico were to be

00:17:16.820 --> 00:17:21.020
significantly reduced, even by a few percentage points,

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:25.100
the ripple effect wouldn't just hurt the quarterly profits of the insurance companies.

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:26.740
It would be way worse than that.

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:31.540
It would severely immediately destabilize the hospitals and the independent

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:35.820
providers who rely on that $9 billion to keep their emergency rooms open?

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:38.380
Because they use that money to cross-subsidize everything else, right?

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:39.300
Exactly.

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:41.620
The cross-subsidization is vital.

00:17:41.620 --> 00:17:44.860
The higher premiums generated from the Medicare Advantage population

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:48.220
help maintain the broader physical hospital network

00:17:48.220 --> 00:17:52.260
that also serves the much lower funded Medicaid and commercial population.

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:54.140
So without the Medicare Advantage money,

00:17:54.140 --> 00:17:55.580
the infrastructure collapses?

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:56.780
Entirely.

00:17:56.780 --> 00:18:00.540
So since we know for a fact that Advantage and Platino are the undisputed

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:05.060
engine of the market, we need to identify who exactly is behind the wheel here.

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:06.100
We need names.

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:06.980
Yeah.

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:11.260
We are talking about a $9 billion federal pipeline for Medicare

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:14.780
and a $17 billion market overall.

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:18.820
Who are the corporate entities capturing this massive flow of cash?

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:22.820
How big do you have to be to manage billions of dollars in medical risk?

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:25.220
To find out, we have to analyze the market share dominance

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:26.740
detailed in the OCS reports.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:29.900
And what you find is that this market is incredibly concentrated.

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:32.220
It's not a bunch of small mom and pop insurers, is it?

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:35.380
No, it is not a fragmented space with hundreds of small players.

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:39.100
It is dominated by a handful of corporate heavyweights, MCS Advantage,

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:44.100
MMM HealthCare, Triple S, First Medical, Humana, and Planetosluid Menonita.

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:45.900
Let's look at the specific scale of these types.

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:49.700
Managing nearly $5 billion means you are practically functioning

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:51.980
as a sovereign financial entity.

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:56.020
You are running vast IT networks, massive call centers,

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:58.380
and employing armies of clinical reviewers.

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:02.980
Then you have MMM HealthCare, which brought in $3.29 billion

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:07.140
while managing 214,969 covered lives.

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:07.980
Incredible.

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:10.180
And on the Medicaid side of the spectrum,

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:12.780
First Medical dominates the planned vital space,

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:17.180
bringing in $1.62 billion with a massive volume

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:18.700
of individual patients.

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:20.220
And the year-over-year growth rates

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:23.340
we are seeing in the filings are just as intense.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:27.580
Look at the top movers in premium growth from 2024 to 2025.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:28.580
The jumps are huge.

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:33.220
MCS Advantage grew their premium by 31.2% in a single year.

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:36.500
Imagine trying to scale your operations by 30% in 12 months.

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:37.980
Unbelievable stress on the system.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:40.540
MCS Life grew by 16.2%.

00:19:40.540 --> 00:19:43.020
Triple S Salud grew by 12.8%.

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.220
What's fascinating here is the strategic lane picking.

00:19:45.220 --> 00:19:47.420
These companies generally do not try to be everything

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:49.500
to everyone because the margins are too punishing.

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:51.100
They choose a lane where they can achieve

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:53.860
the maximum possible operational scale.

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:56.740
For instance, First Medical and Menonita

00:19:56.740 --> 00:20:00.380
lean heavily into the planned vital, the Medicaid side.

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:02.260
So they focus purely on volume.

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:03.380
Exactly.

00:20:03.380 --> 00:20:05.740
They have optimized their entire corporate structure

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:08.180
to focus on managing massive populations

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:10.500
on lower per member premiums.

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:12.380
They are experts in volume.

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:15.100
While companies like MCS and MMM

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:18.620
put their heavy artillery, their specialized case managers,

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:20.580
their chronic disease algorithms,

00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:23.340
squarely into the Medicare Advantage and Platino space.

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:24.420
Yes.

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:26.140
And this level of market dominance

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:28.140
is not just a clever business strategy.

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:30.780
It is a strict prerequisite for survival.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:33.180
Because of that $440 average we talked about.

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:34.060
Precisely.

00:20:34.060 --> 00:20:36.340
Because the per patient premium in Puerto Rico

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:39.500
is so incredibly low compared to the mainland,

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:41.740
the only way a health insurance company can survive

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:43.980
is by moving incredible volume.

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:44.820
Right.

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:47.140
You need massive scale to have the negotiating power

00:20:47.140 --> 00:20:48.380
to build provider networks.

00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:51.140
You need scale to spread your fixed administrative overhead.

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:53.260
You need scale to absorb the statistical risk

00:20:53.260 --> 00:20:56.380
of catastrophic million-dollar ICU claims.

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:58.940
So this isn't exactly a startup-friendly environment,

00:20:58.940 --> 00:20:59.780
is it?

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:02.220
You can't just launch a boutique health insurance company

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:05.020
in Puerto Rico, sign up a couple thousand patients,

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:06.100
and hope for the best.

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:08.180
You would be wiped out instantly.

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:10.340
It's a game of absolute titans.

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:12.300
You either move billions of dollars,

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:13.700
or you get crushed under the weight

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:15.420
of your own administrative costs.

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:19.460
The barrier to entry isn't just regulatory red tape.

00:21:19.460 --> 00:21:21.020
It's purely mathematical.

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:22.820
The math is unforgiving.

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:24.900
If you don't have the sheer volume,

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:29.100
you simply cannot make the math work on $440 a month.

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:31.380
And that harsh reality is reflected

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:33.100
in the steady consolidation we've

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:34.940
seen on the island over the years.

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:37.860
The market forces demand extreme efficiency.

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:39.660
And efficiency at these suffocating price

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:41.580
points demands immense scale.

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:43.540
But here is the ultimate paradox

00:21:43.540 --> 00:21:45.020
of this entire deep dive.

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:47.140
You hear about companies moving $4 billion,

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:49.700
$3 billion in premium, and human nature

00:21:49.700 --> 00:21:51.660
makes you assume they must be printing money.

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:53.420
You assume the executives are swimming

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:56.420
in vaults of gold coins like Scrooge McDuck.

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:59.220
But the data tells a completely different story.

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:02.260
Even if you are a titan moving billions of dollars,

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:03.580
it does not mean you are actually

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:05.020
making a dime of profit.

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:07.820
It sounds counterintuitive, but it's true.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:09.380
Let's look at the financial tightrope

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:10.620
these companies are walking.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:12.740
Because when you look at the profitability ratios

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:16.300
for the Core 9 statutory entities in 2025,

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:18.420
it will give you financial whiplash.

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:22.540
We really need to analyze the 2025 Core 9 statutory net

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:23.060
income.

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:26.100
These are the nine primary foundational health

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:28.620
entities reporting their audited financials

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:31.180
to the NAIC and OCS.

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:32.340
Lay it on us.

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:34.420
And the shocking reality is this.

00:22:34.420 --> 00:22:36.220
For all those billions of dollars

00:22:36.220 --> 00:22:39.260
flowing through the system, the aggregate net income

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:42.180
for these massive companies combined was a loss.

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:44.940
A loss of negative $19.8 million.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:46.620
Wait, I need to stop you there and make

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:48.380
sure we are reading the exact same spreadsheet.

00:22:48.380 --> 00:22:48.940
We are.

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:51.260
We just spent 20 minutes explaining that the market took

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:55.300
in over $17 billion, with the Core 9 statutory entities

00:22:55.300 --> 00:22:58.420
representing over $17 billion of that total landscape.

00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:00.580
And you were telling me that at the end of the year,

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:02.980
when all the hospital bills were paid,

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:04.620
all the pharmacy claims settled,

00:23:04.620 --> 00:23:06.220
and all the lights were kept on,

00:23:06.220 --> 00:23:09.020
the entire industry combined was in the red.

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:11.820
They lost nearly $20 million collectively.

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:12.940
Yes.

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:15.020
Out of the nine reporting entities,

00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:18.900
only five were actually profitable in 2025.

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:21.100
The market is incredibly volatile,

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:24.020
and the margin for error is essentially zero.

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:25.580
It is a razor's edge.

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:27.540
Let's contrast the winners and the losers here

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:29.060
because the swings are violent.

00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:32.580
On the winning side, MCS Advantage had the largest gain.

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:36.380
They posted a net income of $101.3 million.

00:23:36.380 --> 00:23:37.540
Which sounds like a lot.

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:38.420
It does.

00:23:38.420 --> 00:23:39.980
Now, to the average person,

00:23:39.980 --> 00:23:42.460
$100 million sounds like a massive profit.

00:23:42.460 --> 00:23:43.300
Exactly.

00:23:43.300 --> 00:23:46.740
But remember, that is on $4.73 billion in premium.

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:47.580
If you do the math,

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:50.060
that is barely a 2.1% profit margin.

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:50.900
Exactly.

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:53.020
If a grocery store operated on a 2% margin,

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:54.020
they'd be sweating.

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:55.780
And then you look at the other side of the ledger

00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:58.260
where the volatility really shows its teeth.

00:23:58.260 --> 00:24:00.220
Triple S Advantage took a brutal loss

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:02.300
of negative $86.6 million.

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:03.860
And negative $86 million.

00:24:03.860 --> 00:24:05.580
Humana Health Plans of Puerto Rico

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:08.980
lost negative $63.2 million.

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:11.100
MMM Healthcare, despite bringing in

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:13.660
over $3 billion in premium,

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:17.420
posted a net loss of negative $8.9 million.

00:24:17.420 --> 00:24:20.020
I'm looking at these numbers and I am just floored.

00:24:20.020 --> 00:24:22.220
I have to push back and ask the practical question here.

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:23.060
Right here.

00:24:23.060 --> 00:24:27.720
Triple S Advantage brought in over $1.5 billion in premiums,

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:29.660
managed the care of all those patients,

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:33.140
and still managed to lose $86 million.

00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:35.060
How does a business lose that much money

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:36.860
in a single year on an island

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:38.940
and keep its doors open the next morning?

00:24:38.940 --> 00:24:40.900
It's the multi-million dollar question.

00:24:40.900 --> 00:24:43.620
Why didn't the hospitals just stop accepting their cards?

00:24:43.620 --> 00:24:46.020
And it comes down to a fundamental misunderstanding

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:48.140
of how insurance accounting works.

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.860
When you ask how they survive an $86 million loss,

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:52.420
you have to look beyond the income statement

00:24:52.420 --> 00:24:53.540
and look at the balance sheet.

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:54.800
Oh, okay, so reserves.

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:55.640
Yes.

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:58.580
These companies are backed by massive corporate parents

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:01.100
or they have built up significant capital surplus

00:25:01.100 --> 00:25:02.700
over decades of operation.

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:04.460
So they dip into savings.

00:25:04.460 --> 00:25:05.980
When they take a loss like this,

00:25:06.020 --> 00:25:09.180
they survive by drawing down on their historical surplus

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:11.620
or by receiving emergency capital injections

00:25:11.620 --> 00:25:13.620
from their mainland parent companies

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:15.940
or through complex reinsurance treaties

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:18.340
where they have essentially bought insurance for themselves

00:25:18.340 --> 00:25:20.100
against catastrophic losses.

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:23.180
So they are bleeding their savings accounts dry

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:26.240
just to fulfill their promises to the patients.

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:29.300
But why did they lose so much money in the first place?

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:32.780
How do you miscalculate $86 million?

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:34.580
If we connect this to the bigger picture,

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:36.500
it comes down to the brutal friction

00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:38.620
between estimating medical costs

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:40.660
and managing administrative expenses.

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:41.780
The estimation is hard, yeah.

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:43.820
The companies have to predict the future.

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:46.940
They use actuaries to calculate what they call IBNR,

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:49.340
incurred, but not reported claims.

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:51.500
Okay, give me an example of IBNR.

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:53.620
Imagine a patient who spent three weeks

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:55.340
in the ICU in November.

00:25:55.340 --> 00:25:58.540
The hospital might not actually send the final audited bill

00:25:58.540 --> 00:26:00.780
to the insurance company until February.

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:03.540
The insurance company has to estimate, in December,

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:05.540
exactly how much money they think they owe

00:26:05.540 --> 00:26:07.380
for that patient and reserve it.

00:26:07.380 --> 00:26:10.420
If their actuaries are wrong by even one or 2%

00:26:10.420 --> 00:26:12.820
across hundreds of thousands of patients,

00:26:12.820 --> 00:26:16.060
a projected $10 million profit

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:18.860
instantly becomes a $40 million loss

00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:21.060
when the real bills finally arrive.

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:23.020
Man, and that's just the medical side.

00:26:23.020 --> 00:26:24.260
What about the operational side?

00:26:24.260 --> 00:26:26.060
You can't just blame the hospitals for everything.

00:26:26.060 --> 00:26:26.940
Exactly.

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:30.060
When your margins are kept by low federal funding rates,

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:33.940
your internal corporate efficiency has to be flawless.

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:36.740
Let's look at the administrative expense ratios in the data.

00:26:36.740 --> 00:26:37.860
What exactly does that measure?

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:40.140
This is the percentage of premiums spent strictly

00:26:40.140 --> 00:26:42.380
on running the company, paying the staff,

00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:45.660
upgrading the IT systems, paying rent for the offices,

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:47.060
marketing, all of that,

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:49.660
before paying a single doctor for actual healthcare.

00:26:49.660 --> 00:26:51.260
Okay, looking at the data,

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:54.540
MCS Advantage, the one that actually made a 2% profit,

00:26:54.540 --> 00:26:57.820
ran at an administrative ratio of 8.7%.

00:26:57.860 --> 00:27:00.860
3S Solud, which is their commercial and Medicaid arm,

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:03.660
ran at an incredibly lean 6.9%.

00:27:03.660 --> 00:27:04.660
Extremely lean.

00:27:04.660 --> 00:27:06.500
But then you look at Humana,

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:09.980
their administrative ratio ran at 17.2%.

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:12.100
And there is the direct correlation.

00:27:12.100 --> 00:27:14.740
When the overall market margin is this thin,

00:27:14.740 --> 00:27:17.020
an administrative blow of 17%

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:19.340
leads straight off a financial cliff.

00:27:19.340 --> 00:27:20.180
Right, into the red.

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:24.060
For Humana, that 17.2% admin ratio

00:27:24.100 --> 00:27:28.140
contributed heavily to a $63 million deficit.

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:30.300
You simply cannot spend 17 cents

00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:32.820
of every single dollar on administration

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:34.300
when the medical claims from the hospitals

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:36.860
require 85 to 90 cents of that dollar.

00:27:36.860 --> 00:27:39.060
The math breaks, you run out of pennies.

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:40.900
It also shows how deeply precarious

00:27:40.900 --> 00:27:43.940
the pricing and claim estimations are year over year.

00:27:43.940 --> 00:27:45.420
Look at first medical in the filings.

00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:46.780
Yeah, that's a wild swing.

00:27:46.780 --> 00:27:50.860
In 2024, they swung a nearly negative $40 million loss.

00:27:50.860 --> 00:27:53.380
But in 2025, they managed to write the ship

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:56.460
and post a $5.3 million profit.

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:59.500
That is a $45 million swing in exactly 12 months.

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:00.940
It's like trying to land a jumbo jet

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:02.540
on a postage stamp during a hurricane.

00:28:02.540 --> 00:28:05.020
Seriously, if a new, incredibly costly

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:06.740
specialty drug hits the market,

00:28:06.740 --> 00:28:08.620
or there's an unexpectedly bad flu season

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:11.260
that puts thousands of seniors in the hospital,

00:28:11.260 --> 00:28:13.740
a $5 million profit instantly evaporates

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:15.140
into a massive loss.

00:28:15.140 --> 00:28:18.100
It is the definition of a financial tightrope.

00:28:18.100 --> 00:28:19.900
And that exact question you asked earlier,

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:23.020
how does a business lose $86 million

00:28:23.020 --> 00:28:26.100
and keep its doors open without the hospital's panicking

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:29.260
is precisely why regulators do not just trust

00:28:29.260 --> 00:28:31.100
these corporate executives to figure it out

00:28:31.100 --> 00:28:32.340
on their own honor system.

00:28:32.340 --> 00:28:33.740
The government knows better.

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:36.900
The government knows exactly how volatile this market is,

00:28:36.900 --> 00:28:39.100
and they know the catastrophic human cost

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:41.060
if one of these companies actually defaults.

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:42.980
Which means they require massive,

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:45.900
legally binding financial safety nets.

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:48.140
You cannot have a company managing

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:49.460
the life or death healthcare

00:28:49.460 --> 00:28:52.060
of 300,000 vulnerable seniors

00:28:52.100 --> 00:28:54.220
suddenly go bankrupt and turn the lights off

00:28:54.220 --> 00:28:55.380
on a Tuesday afternoon.

00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:57.620
No, that would be a humanitarian crisis.

00:28:57.620 --> 00:29:00.460
So let's transition and dig into the liquidity,

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:01.740
the physical guarantees,

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:03.660
and how these companies must legally prove

00:29:03.660 --> 00:29:05.220
they won't collapse.

00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:07.260
Let's talk about financial compliance.

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:09.660
The financial stability and compliance requirements

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:11.780
mandated by the National Association

00:29:11.780 --> 00:29:14.500
of Insurance Commissioners, the NAIC,

00:29:14.500 --> 00:29:17.140
and the Puerto Rico OCS are incredibly strict.

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:18.140
They don't mess around.

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:19.820
They are meticulously designed

00:29:19.820 --> 00:29:21.940
to prevent systemic failure.

00:29:21.940 --> 00:29:24.220
To understand how they keep the market stable,

00:29:24.220 --> 00:29:26.460
we need to introduce a few specific terms

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:27.700
from the financial filings

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:29.740
that dictate a company's survival.

00:29:29.740 --> 00:29:31.180
Okay, let's unpack these terms

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:33.420
because they are absolutely crucial.

00:29:33.420 --> 00:29:37.860
In the OCS reports, we see the term actidos admitidos,

00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:40.380
which translates to admitted assets.

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:41.940
Then there's total depasivos,

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:43.660
which are total liabilities.

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:45.540
And finally, the most important one,

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:47.300
capital E accidente,

00:29:47.300 --> 00:29:50.260
which translates to capital and surplus.

00:29:50.260 --> 00:29:52.980
Let's define the friction between those terms for you.

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:54.700
Total liabilities are everything

00:29:54.700 --> 00:29:56.460
the insurance company legally owes.

00:29:56.460 --> 00:29:57.300
The bills.

00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:59.500
Primarily, this is the estimated medical claims

00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:00.620
they know they have to pay,

00:30:00.620 --> 00:30:03.260
plus those IB&R reserves we talked about earlier

00:30:03.260 --> 00:30:04.580
for claims that have happened,

00:30:04.580 --> 00:30:05.700
but haven't been billed yet.

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:06.540
Got it.

00:30:06.540 --> 00:30:08.100
Admitted assets, on the other hand,

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:11.060
are the highly liquid, incredibly secure funds

00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:12.540
the company actually has on hand

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:14.300
to pay those bills right now.

00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:17.340
We are talking about cash, stable government bonds,

00:30:17.340 --> 00:30:19.220
things they can liquidate tomorrow morning.

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:22.100
So they don't count the fancy office furniture,

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:23.740
the CEO's company car,

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:25.620
or the branding value of their logo.

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:26.460
Exactly.

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:29.140
The regulators do not care about your real estate investments

00:30:29.140 --> 00:30:30.980
if they take six months to sell.

00:30:30.980 --> 00:30:32.420
They only care about assets

00:30:32.420 --> 00:30:35.660
that can instantly reliably pay a hospital bill today.

00:30:35.660 --> 00:30:36.500
Makes sense.

00:30:36.500 --> 00:30:38.700
And capital and surplus is the ultimate cushion.

00:30:38.700 --> 00:30:40.780
It is the mathematical difference,

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:44.020
the admitted assets minus the total liabilities.

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:45.500
So just how much buffer they have.

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:48.180
It is the absolute unvarnished measure

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:50.300
of a company's financial health.

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:52.300
If that surplus number drops below

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:55.140
a certain strictly calculated regulatory threshold

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:58.140
known as the risk-based capital requirement,

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:00.420
the OCS steps in, seizes control,

00:31:00.420 --> 00:31:02.940
and essentially puts the company in receivership.

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:04.500
But the regulators go even further

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:06.820
than just looking at a spreadsheet once a quarter.

00:31:06.820 --> 00:31:09.740
They demand actual physical guarantees.

00:31:09.740 --> 00:31:13.580
The data highlights the depositoestapatorio in Puerto Rico,

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:14.780
the statutory deposit.

00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:17.420
Yes, the literal physical guarantee companies

00:31:17.460 --> 00:31:19.140
must lock away with the government.

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:20.060
Yeah.

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:22.220
They are required to place millions of dollars

00:31:22.220 --> 00:31:24.500
in a restricted account that they cannot touch.

00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:27.580
For example, looking at the foreign insurers in the data,

00:31:27.580 --> 00:31:31.060
AIG is required to hold $2.6 million

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:32.340
in a statutory deposit.

00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:33.860
And they can't touch it.

00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:37.220
Riverstone holds $3.5 million.

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:40.020
Medco holds $2.5 million.

00:31:40.020 --> 00:31:42.580
And the massive domestic health organizations

00:31:42.580 --> 00:31:45.740
have their own incredibly strict deposit

00:31:45.740 --> 00:31:47.180
and reserve requirements

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:49.780
based on their specific premium volume.

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:52.460
It's essentially a financial hostage situation.

00:31:52.460 --> 00:31:53.300
Yeah.

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:54.980
The government looks at these massive corporations

00:31:54.980 --> 00:31:56.780
and says, you can operate here.

00:31:56.780 --> 00:31:59.380
You can take these billions of dollars in premiums

00:31:59.380 --> 00:32:01.420
from our citizens and the federal government,

00:32:01.420 --> 00:32:04.220
but we are locking millions of your own cash in our vault

00:32:04.220 --> 00:32:05.380
just in case you mess up.

00:32:05.380 --> 00:32:07.460
It forces fiscal responsibility

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:09.900
by keeping their feet continuously to the fire.

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:10.860
Exactly.

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:13.060
If you decide you're tired of losing money

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:14.380
and you pack up and leave the island

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:15.900
without paying the doctors,

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:17.420
the government already has your deposit

00:32:17.420 --> 00:32:19.100
to start making the providers whole.

00:32:19.100 --> 00:32:22.340
Furthermore, Puerto Rico has entirely unique geographic

00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:24.300
and climatic vulnerabilities,

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:27.540
which brings us to a very specific regulation,

00:32:27.540 --> 00:32:30.700
the catastrophe reserve requirements under chapter 25

00:32:30.700 --> 00:32:32.660
of the Puerto Rico insurance code.

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:33.500
Right.

00:32:33.500 --> 00:32:34.580
Because when you are an island

00:32:34.580 --> 00:32:35.660
in the middle of the Caribbean,

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:38.020
you don't just have to plan for bad flu seasons.

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:40.420
You have to plan for hurricanes

00:32:40.420 --> 00:32:41.700
tearing the infrastructure apart.

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:42.740
Exactly.

00:32:42.740 --> 00:32:45.340
Rule 72 of the insurance code mandates

00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:46.860
that certain insurers build

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:50.620
and maintain a specific catastrophic reserve fund.

00:32:50.620 --> 00:32:52.180
This is money designed to protect

00:32:52.180 --> 00:32:55.980
against massive systemic island-wide shocks.

00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:56.820
Like Maria.

00:32:56.820 --> 00:32:57.660
Think about what happens

00:32:57.660 --> 00:32:59.660
when a category five hurricane hits.

00:32:59.660 --> 00:33:00.980
The power grid goes down.

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:03.220
Hospitals are forced to run on diesel generators,

00:33:03.220 --> 00:33:04.900
which costs an absolute fortune.

00:33:04.900 --> 00:33:05.740
Right.

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:07.540
And medical needs spike due to injuries

00:33:07.540 --> 00:33:09.820
while simultaneously the standard infrastructure

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:11.980
to process claims and authorized treatments

00:33:11.980 --> 00:33:13.500
is completely damaged.

00:33:13.500 --> 00:33:15.700
Claims become highly unpredictable.

00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:18.060
The regulators force the companies to hoard capital

00:33:18.060 --> 00:33:20.380
specifically for those black swan events,

00:33:20.380 --> 00:33:21.820
ensuring that even in the dark,

00:33:21.820 --> 00:33:23.980
the money is there to pay for trauma care.

00:33:23.980 --> 00:33:26.060
So let's sort of summarize the reality

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:29.420
of an insurance executive in Puerto Rico right now.

00:33:29.420 --> 00:33:31.540
You are managing federal funds

00:33:31.540 --> 00:33:34.980
that pay drastically less than the mainland rate.

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:36.060
Drastically less.

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:39.140
You are operating on a 2% profit margin

00:33:39.140 --> 00:33:40.260
if you are lucky,

00:33:40.340 --> 00:33:43.100
or actively losing tens of millions of dollars

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:45.340
if you make a single miscalculation.

00:33:46.180 --> 00:33:47.900
And while all this is happening,

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:49.900
the government is forcing you

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:52.500
to lock up millions in cash reserves

00:33:52.500 --> 00:33:54.260
just to keep your license active.

00:33:54.260 --> 00:33:56.540
It is a wildly punishing environment.

00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:57.580
It sounds impossible.

00:33:57.580 --> 00:33:59.580
But it is punishing by design.

00:33:59.580 --> 00:34:01.380
These strict solvency regulations

00:34:01.380 --> 00:34:04.940
are designed to ruthlessly weed out weak players.

00:34:04.940 --> 00:34:06.820
If you cannot maintain your surplus,

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:08.980
if you mismanage your administrative ratio

00:34:08.980 --> 00:34:10.180
like we saw earlier,

00:34:10.180 --> 00:34:11.900
the insurance commissioner has the authority

00:34:11.900 --> 00:34:14.020
and the absolute mandate to step in,

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:15.340
take control of your operations

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:16.340
and protect the patients

00:34:16.340 --> 00:34:17.860
and the providers from your failure.

00:34:17.860 --> 00:34:20.060
So the system prioritizes the stability

00:34:20.060 --> 00:34:21.220
of the hospital network

00:34:21.220 --> 00:34:23.300
above the profit margins of the insurers.

00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:24.220
100%.

00:34:24.220 --> 00:34:26.100
Okay, so we have established

00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:28.460
that the companies are highly regulated.

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:30.500
We know they are moving billions of dollars

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:32.620
but operating on razor thin margins.

00:34:32.620 --> 00:34:34.700
We know some are losing tens of millions of dollars

00:34:34.700 --> 00:34:36.460
a year, but this brings us

00:34:36.460 --> 00:34:38.060
to the most important part of the puzzle.

00:34:38.060 --> 00:34:39.300
The ultimate metric.

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:41.340
Where is all that money actually going?

00:34:42.260 --> 00:34:43.940
If they aren't keeping it as profit

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:46.820
and they are forced to hold vast amounts in reserves,

00:34:46.820 --> 00:34:49.580
how much is actually reaching the patients?

00:34:49.580 --> 00:34:51.060
To definitively find out

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:52.980
if these are just cold medicine plans

00:34:52.980 --> 00:34:54.700
like Dr. Ramos said,

00:34:54.700 --> 00:34:56.780
we have to look at the undeniable metric

00:34:56.780 --> 00:34:58.380
in healthcare finance.

00:34:58.380 --> 00:35:00.940
The medical loss ratio or MLR?

00:35:00.940 --> 00:35:02.020
The medical loss ratio

00:35:02.020 --> 00:35:04.380
is the absolute core of this entire analysis.

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:06.780
It is the inescapable truth of the data.

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:07.700
Let's get into it.

00:35:07.700 --> 00:35:10.380
The MLR is the percentage of premium dollars

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:12.340
that an insurance company spends directly

00:35:12.340 --> 00:35:14.500
on medical claims and quality improvements

00:35:14.500 --> 00:35:17.140
rather than administrative costs and profits.

00:35:17.140 --> 00:35:19.660
And we have a critical non-negotiable rule

00:35:19.660 --> 00:35:21.900
when analyzing this data for Puerto Rico.

00:35:21.900 --> 00:35:23.340
Yes, listen closely here

00:35:23.340 --> 00:35:26.740
because this is where a lot of analysts get the math wrong.

00:35:26.740 --> 00:35:29.540
When discussing the MLR for Medicare Advantage,

00:35:29.540 --> 00:35:33.020
you must add both the standard Medicare Advantage

00:35:33.020 --> 00:35:36.580
and D, the Medicare Advantage Platino numbers together.

00:35:36.580 --> 00:35:38.740
You cannot separate one from the other

00:35:38.740 --> 00:35:39.900
for the medical loss ratio.

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:42.380
You absolutely cannot make that assumption

00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:45.700
because operationally, demographically, and financially,

00:35:45.700 --> 00:35:47.620
they are managed as a unified,

00:35:47.620 --> 00:35:50.140
singular risk pool by these companies.

00:35:50.140 --> 00:35:52.100
Let's walk through the exact math for you

00:35:52.100 --> 00:35:55.020
using the audited 2025 numbers.

00:35:55.020 --> 00:35:56.900
We take the combined Platino premium,

00:35:56.900 --> 00:35:59.660
which was $5.73 billion.

00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:00.500
Okay.

00:36:00.500 --> 00:36:02.300
We add the standard Medicare Advantage premium,

00:36:02.300 --> 00:36:04.900
which was $3.27 billion.

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:06.820
That gives us a combined premium pool

00:36:06.820 --> 00:36:09.700
of approximately $9.0 billion.

00:36:09.700 --> 00:36:11.420
Now, for the most important number,

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:12.900
the claims actually paid out.

00:36:12.900 --> 00:36:16.220
The combined Platino claims were $5.14 billion.

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:19.740
The standard Medicare Advantage claims were $2.86 billion.

00:36:19.740 --> 00:36:21.020
Which means together,

00:36:21.020 --> 00:36:23.700
that is roughly $8.0 billion

00:36:23.700 --> 00:36:25.460
physically paid out to the hospitals,

00:36:25.460 --> 00:36:27.540
the doctors, and the pharmacies on the island.

00:36:27.540 --> 00:36:30.220
So when you divide that $8.0 billion in claims

00:36:30.220 --> 00:36:32.500
by the 9.8 billion in premium,

00:36:32.500 --> 00:36:35.460
the combined MLR for the Medicare space in Puerto Rico

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:36.940
is nearly 89%.

00:36:36.940 --> 00:36:37.860
89%.

00:36:37.860 --> 00:36:39.140
Let's look at the overall market.

00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:40.460
Across all lines of business,

00:36:40.460 --> 00:36:43.620
in 2025, commercial, Medicaid, Medicare,

00:36:43.620 --> 00:36:47.060
there was $15.49 billion in claims paid

00:36:47.060 --> 00:36:50.180
out of $17.18 billion in premium.

00:36:50.180 --> 00:36:53.060
The overall market, MLR, was 90.2%.

00:36:53.060 --> 00:36:55.980
And if you isolate Plan Vital, the Medicaid population,

00:36:55.980 --> 00:36:58.140
the numbers become even more extreme.

00:36:58.140 --> 00:36:58.980
Oh man, yeah.

00:36:59.060 --> 00:37:00.340
The MLR for Plan Vital

00:37:00.340 --> 00:37:03.780
hit an astronomical 95.8% market-wide

00:37:03.780 --> 00:37:07.260
and 95.5% within the Core 9 reporting entities.

00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:09.620
Let me stop and just absorb the reality of that number.

00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:11.020
95.8%.

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:12.900
Are they actually too efficient for their own good?

00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:13.980
It's a valid question.

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:15.700
I want you to imagine running a restaurant

00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:18.500
and 95.8 cents of every single dollar

00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:19.780
a customer hands you

00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:22.300
has to be spent exclusively on the raw ingredients,

00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:23.460
the meat, and the vegetables.

00:37:23.460 --> 00:37:24.460
Nothing left over.

00:37:24.460 --> 00:37:26.980
That leaves you less than five cents

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:28.020
to pay the chef,

00:37:28.020 --> 00:37:28.860
pay the waiters,

00:37:28.860 --> 00:37:29.820
pay the electric bill,

00:37:29.820 --> 00:37:30.660
pay the rent,

00:37:30.660 --> 00:37:32.780
and somehow eke out a profit.

00:37:32.780 --> 00:37:33.940
It's insane.

00:37:33.940 --> 00:37:36.860
If they are paying out 90 to 95 cents on the dollar

00:37:36.860 --> 00:37:38.380
for actual medical care

00:37:38.380 --> 00:37:40.140
and using the rest for their administration,

00:37:40.140 --> 00:37:41.220
which we saw earlier,

00:37:41.220 --> 00:37:43.100
is around seven to 10%.

00:37:43.100 --> 00:37:44.460
For the efficient companies there

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:46.540
is practically zero margin for error.

00:37:46.540 --> 00:37:47.820
There is nothing left.

00:37:47.820 --> 00:37:50.700
That is the exact math that shatters the myth.

00:37:50.700 --> 00:37:52.740
I wanna break down what an 89%

00:37:52.740 --> 00:37:56.820
or a 95% MLR actually means in the real world

00:37:56.820 --> 00:37:58.100
to the patients on the ground.

00:37:58.100 --> 00:37:58.940
What does it mean?

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:01.340
It means that for every single dollar of premium,

00:38:01.340 --> 00:38:02.700
the federal or local government

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:04.220
pays the insurance company,

00:38:04.220 --> 00:38:07.140
89 to 95 cents goes directly immediately

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:08.940
out the door to pay the doctors,

00:38:08.940 --> 00:38:10.660
to keep the hospital beds open,

00:38:10.660 --> 00:38:13.340
to pay for life-saving cardiovascular surgeries,

00:38:13.340 --> 00:38:15.980
and to pay for highly complex pharmacy drugs.

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:17.300
And this brings us right back

00:38:17.300 --> 00:38:18.660
to the beginning of the show.

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:21.580
To Dr. Victor Ramos' famous cynical quote,

00:38:21.580 --> 00:38:24.660
Plans que solo cubren medicinas de cataro.

00:38:24.660 --> 00:38:27.580
If a health plan truly only covered cold medicine,

00:38:27.580 --> 00:38:30.340
what would its MLR actually look like on a spreadsheet?

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:32.460
It would be functionally microscopic.

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:35.540
If a plan truly only covered Advil and Musenax,

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:37.540
the MLR would be incredibly low.

00:38:37.540 --> 00:38:38.900
Like what, maybe 20%?

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:41.380
Maybe 20 or 30% at best.

00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:43.500
Because cold medicine is incredibly cheap

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:45.340
compared to actual medical care.

00:38:45.340 --> 00:38:48.700
The insurance company would collect their $440 a month,

00:38:48.700 --> 00:38:50.860
pay out $15 for cough syrup,

00:38:50.980 --> 00:38:52.900
and pocket the rest as massive,

00:38:52.900 --> 00:38:55.460
unprecedented corporate profit.

00:38:55.460 --> 00:38:59.420
But that is undeniably not what is happening.

00:38:59.420 --> 00:39:00.260
Not even close.

00:39:00.260 --> 00:39:03.700
The fact that the MLR is sitting at 90% or higher definitively

00:39:03.700 --> 00:39:05.300
mathematically proves that these plans

00:39:05.300 --> 00:39:06.820
are not just buying cold medicine.

00:39:06.820 --> 00:39:08.180
They're paying out billions.

00:39:08.180 --> 00:39:11.140
$15.49 billion to be exact.

00:39:11.140 --> 00:39:14.780
For chronic care, oncology, cardiology, ICU stays,

00:39:14.780 --> 00:39:17.220
and the most critical treatments available in modern medicine,

00:39:17.220 --> 00:39:20.100
you do not hit $15 billion by handing out cough drops.

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:22.060
And we can even look at the very latest data

00:39:22.060 --> 00:39:24.580
to see this high efficiency trend continuing.

00:39:24.580 --> 00:39:26.620
What does Q1-2026 look like?

00:39:26.620 --> 00:39:29.420
Looking at the Q1-2026 data,

00:39:29.420 --> 00:39:31.540
the market MLR cooled slightly,

00:39:31.540 --> 00:39:35.660
but remains incredibly high at 83.1%.

00:39:35.660 --> 00:39:38.940
That represents $3.76 billion in claims

00:39:38.940 --> 00:39:42.140
paid out on $4.52 billion in premium

00:39:42.140 --> 00:39:45.220
in just the first three months of the year alone.

00:39:45.220 --> 00:39:47.660
The money never stops flowing to the providers.

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:50.100
So these aren't cheap plans delivering nothing.

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:52.220
That is a total misconception.

00:39:52.220 --> 00:39:54.980
They are incredibly ruthlessly efficient plans

00:39:54.980 --> 00:39:57.860
delivering almost everything on an impossibly tight budget.

00:39:57.860 --> 00:39:58.700
Yes.

00:39:58.700 --> 00:40:01.260
The companies aren't hoarding the money in offshore accounts.

00:40:01.260 --> 00:40:02.660
The money is flowing directly,

00:40:02.660 --> 00:40:05.340
constantly into the Puerto Rican healthcare infrastructure.

00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:07.020
This brings us back to the fundamental question

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:09.300
we started with at the very beginning of this deep dive.

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:11.820
Do these $440 a month plans

00:40:11.820 --> 00:40:14.020
actually take care of the people of Puerto Rico?

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:15.340
Based strictly on the data,

00:40:15.340 --> 00:40:16.860
the billions of audited data points

00:40:16.860 --> 00:40:19.020
processed by the OCS and the NAIC,

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:21.220
the story of the Plains Medicals to Catarro

00:40:21.220 --> 00:40:22.540
is a complete myth.

00:40:22.540 --> 00:40:24.060
It's mathematically untrue.

00:40:24.060 --> 00:40:26.300
It is a powerful political sandbite,

00:40:26.300 --> 00:40:29.780
but it is fundamentally mathematically untrue.

00:40:29.780 --> 00:40:31.380
Looking strictly at the numbers,

00:40:31.380 --> 00:40:34.220
these companies are delivering massive critical services

00:40:34.220 --> 00:40:35.900
to the population of Puerto Rico,

00:40:35.900 --> 00:40:39.180
paying out over $15 billion in claims

00:40:39.180 --> 00:40:41.420
to providers in a single year.

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:43.900
This data is a powerful message.

00:40:43.900 --> 00:40:45.340
It is a message to the government

00:40:45.340 --> 00:40:46.860
and the insurance commissioner

00:40:46.860 --> 00:40:49.900
to strictly safeguard the financial solvency

00:40:49.900 --> 00:40:53.660
and fiscal responsibility of this highly fragile system.

00:40:53.660 --> 00:40:55.540
It's a message to everyone involved.

00:40:55.540 --> 00:40:56.980
It is a message to the providers

00:40:56.980 --> 00:40:58.580
and the insurance companies

00:40:58.580 --> 00:41:01.220
that they must maintain this delicate balance

00:41:01.220 --> 00:41:04.100
because there is absolutely no room for waste.

00:41:04.100 --> 00:41:05.500
And it is a message to the people

00:41:05.500 --> 00:41:08.220
and to you, our listeners who buy health insurance,

00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:10.300
that the system, though financially strained

00:41:10.300 --> 00:41:12.420
by massive federal funding disparities

00:41:12.420 --> 00:41:13.900
compared to the mainland,

00:41:13.940 --> 00:41:17.020
is functioning at maximum efficiency to deliver care.

00:41:17.020 --> 00:41:18.060
As we leave you today,

00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:20.260
I want to offer one final provocative thought

00:41:20.260 --> 00:41:21.340
for you to mull over.

00:41:21.340 --> 00:41:23.220
We saw in the federal context data

00:41:23.220 --> 00:41:27.380
that CMS, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services,

00:41:27.380 --> 00:41:30.020
is currently looking at the 2027 Rate Book

00:41:30.020 --> 00:41:32.460
for Puerto Rico's municipalities.

00:41:32.460 --> 00:41:34.300
Every year, the federal government decides

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:36.860
how much to adjust the funding

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:39.140
for these Medicare Advantage plans.

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:40.340
If the federal government decides

00:41:40.340 --> 00:41:43.340
to tighten those rates, even a fraction of a percent,

00:41:43.340 --> 00:41:45.740
what happens to a market that is already running

00:41:45.740 --> 00:41:47.900
on a 90% medical loss ratio

00:41:47.900 --> 00:41:50.700
and a collective negative profit margin?

00:41:50.700 --> 00:41:52.500
Where does the system find the money

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:55.580
when there are no more efficiencies left to squeeze?

00:41:55.580 --> 00:41:57.180
That's a terrifying question.

00:41:57.180 --> 00:42:00.660
You hand a system $440 and ask for a miracle.

00:42:00.660 --> 00:42:02.900
Against all odds, they give you a miracle.

00:42:02.900 --> 00:42:06.180
But what happens next year when you hand them 430?

00:42:06.180 --> 00:42:07.100
So what does this all mean?

00:42:07.100 --> 00:42:09.340
I want to make this as clear as I possibly can

00:42:09.340 --> 00:42:11.620
based on everything we have unpacked today.

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:13.500
Medicare Advantage is paying the bills

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:15.420
for healthcare in Puerto Rico.

